{"name":"Candle in the Wind","hex":"#f9ebbf","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"candle in the wind","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fragile or vulnerable thing, likely to be put in jeopardy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/candle_in_the_wind"}],"entries":[{"title":"Candle in the Wind","blocks":[{"text":"\"Candle in the Wind\" is a song written by British musician Elton John and lyricist Bernie Taupin, and performed by John. It was originally written in 1973, in honour of Marilyn Monroe, who had died 11 years earlier."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/3e/Candle_in_the_Wind_by_Elton_John_1973_UK_single_solid_centre.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Candle_in_the_Wind","wikidata":"Q56071967"},{"title":"Candle in the Wind (play)","blocks":[{"text":"Candle in the Wind was a 1941 Broadway three-act drama written by Maxwell Anderson, \nproduced by the Theatre Guild and the Playwrights' Company and directed by Alfred Lunt. Jo Mielziner \ncreated the scenic and lighting design. It ran for 95 performances from October 22, 1941 to January 10, 1942 at the \nShubert Theatre as a part of the 1941-1942 play season. It was included in Burns Mantle's The Best Plays of 1941-1942."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Candle_in_the_Wind_(play)","wikidata":"Q16385210"},{"title":"Candle","blocks":[{"text":"A candle is an ignitable wick embedded in wax, or another flammable solid substance such as tallow, that provides light, and in some cases, a fragrance. A candle can also provide heat or a method of keeping time. Candles have been used for over two millennia around the world, and were a significant form of indoor lighting until the invention of other types of light sources. Although electric light has largely made candle use nonessential for illumination, candles are still commonly used for functional, symbolic and aesthetic purposes and in specific cultural and religious settings."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/LA2_Skultuna_kontorsljusstake.jpg/330px-LA2_Skultuna_kontorsljusstake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Candle","wikidata":"Q12888135"},{"title":"Wind","blocks":[{"text":"Wind is the natural movement of air or other gases relative to a planet's surface. Winds occur on a range of scales, from thunderstorm flows lasting tens of minutes, to local breezes generated by heating of land surfaces and lasting a few hours, to global winds resulting from the difference in absorption of solar energy between the climate zones on Earth. The study of wind is known as anemology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif/330px-Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind","wikidata":"Q8094"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/07/Elton_John_-_Candle_in_the_Wind_%281986%29.jpg/250px-Elton_John_-_Candle_in_the_Wind_%281986%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/07/Elton_John_-_Candle_in_the_Wind_%281986%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/07/Elton_John_-_Candle_in_the_Wind_%281986%29.jpg/250px-Elton_John_-_Candle_in_the_Wind_%281986%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Single release of the 1986 live version","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AElton_John_-_Candle_in_the_Wind_(1986).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Always.gif/330px-Always.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_c